instance, you can place the invocation shown above in a Makefile, so
that only running c`make` is needed to produce the result.

Concept: debugger

If you prefer a more graphical user interface, you can use the Gamma
Debugger, henceforth referred to as the def:Debugger.  The Debugger is
in essence a geometry viewing application, but it posseses some
features more common to a debugger, which make it particularly
convenient to use with a system like Gamma.  It also posseses a
similar interface.

Using the Debugger is more appropriate when developing a model, where
you would typically want to inspect the current results of your
program, make neccessary changes and repeat until the desired output
is achieved.  The simplest way to run the program contained in
f`cube.ext` through the Debugger, is by starting it as shown below.

```
gammadb --target cube --args cube.ext
```

This will start the debugger, which will print some introductory
messages, followed by the prompt s`#`.  The o`--target` and o`--args`
options provide the minimal configuration needed to run the program
and display the single output contained in it and will be fully
explained later.

You can now type the command c`run` at the prompt, which will invoke
Gamma as needed to compile the model and send it to the Debugger,
where it will be loaded and displayed in a window. Use the mouse to
rotate, pan, or zoom it by dragging on the window with the left, right
and middle mouse buttons respectively.

If you were now to decide that the cube isn't rounded quite enough,
you could simply edit f`cube.ext` and change the sphere radius from
s`7/10` to s`2/3` and repeat the run command^[You can do this by
simply pressing return, which is equivalent to entering the last
command again.].  The displayed model should change accordingly.

When you've finished working, enter the command c`exit`, or c`quit`,
or press k`Ctrl-D` to exit the Debugger.

