## Designing a Sprocket

Being a toothed wheel, a sprocket is defined in terms of the disk from
which it is machined and the geometry of the parts that need to be cut
out from its rim to form the teeth.  These are called the "tooth gap
forms" in the standard and are based on two curves.

The roots of the gaps are formed by segments of the small circles
shown in ref:sprocket-diagram, subtending a specified angle, called
the "roller-seating angle" in the standard.  From there, arcs along
the larger circles are followed to form the flanks of the teeth, up to
the tips.

After calculating a number of derived quantities following the
formulas laid out in the standard^[For the tip diameter, a value
halfway between the smallest and largest diameters allowed in the
standard is used.] the program defines a utlity function that places
its argument relative to the roller seating curves.  Such placement
functions are very useful in practice, because they exploit symmetries
in a design.  This one first rotates the given shape to align it with
tooth `n`, then translates it `r` units below the center of the
corresponding roller seating circle and rotates it by a fraction `c`
of the roller-seating angle relative to that center.  It is used to
make a polygon out of the seat-to-flank transition points of each
tooth, by taking the convex hull of these points.  The result is shown
in dotted lines in ref:sprocket-diagram.

From it the roller seats are cut out, by taking the difference with
circles placed using the same function.  This produces the central
part of the sprocket, shown in the bottom left frame of fig: sprocket.

The circles forming the flanks are tangent to the roller seating
curves, so the placement function can be used once more to transform
them into place^[The circle is translated vertically until its top is
tangent with the bottom of the corresponing roller seat curve and then
rotated by half the roller-seating angle.].  The sprocket teeth can
then be formed by taking appropriate intersections of these circles
and the sprocket's rim circle.  They are shown in the top left frame
of fig: sprocket.

Figure: sprocket-diagram
```print sprocket_diagram.scm
# resize 500 250
# translate 0.3 10
# zoom 1.3
```
  The curves determining the form of a sprocket.  The roller seating
  curves are made from the small dotted circles, while the larger
  circles form the tooth flank curves.  Together, they determine the
  tooth gap forms, the parts that are cut away from a disk, to produce
  the sprocket.

