## Program Outputs

The final result is the union of these components, extruded^[Extrusion
is a manufacturing process that creates objects of a fixed
cross-sectional profile, as is the case here.  In Gamma this is the
simplest case of extrusion, but the operation is much more general.]
to the specified width. This would normally be followed by further
processing to give the sprocket a proper rim profile as the
specification demands, but this is not done here, to keep the example
from becoming more complicated.

Since this is the end result of the program, it is designated as an
ouput.  This makes it possible to produce a spocket of any
configuration and save it to a file in one of the supported formats.
For example, the following invocation will produce a sprocket with 9
teeth, matching a chain with 4mm diameter rollers and a pitch of 6mm.

```
gamma -Dz=9 -Dd_1=4 -Dp=6 -o sprocket.stl sprocket.scm
```

Besides being written to disk, outputs can also be inspected in the
Debugger.  When the c`run` command is invoked, the Debugger will run
Gamma with outputs enabled for each of the current window's viewport
targets.  If matching outputs are defined in the program, their
geometry is loaded in the corresponding viewports.  Since the window
is divided into three viewports, two of which have their default
targets, "1" and "2", while the third's target is "sprocket", these
are the outputs that will be loaded on each run.

The outputs "1" and "2" are meant for debugging.  In this example,
they are set to the two components that make up the sprocket's
cross-section, but in general you would set them to output the part of
the design you're currently working on.  The `output` function returns
its first argument, so you can usually just wrap any expression that
returns geometry that you'd like to inspect inside an `output` call
and press the k`r` key on the respective viewport.  This is
essentially analogous to "`printf()` debugging".

