# rotate 45 -45 -45
```
  The output, as seen in the Debugger.  The lower left viewport shows
  the thread profile polygon, which is extruded along a helical path,
  forming the threads shown in the upper left viewport.  A shaft and a
  chamfered hexagonal head is added, to form the final bolt shown in
  the right viewport.

The extrusion operation takes a polygon and a list of arbitrary
transformations.  It transforms the polygon with each of the
transformations in order and connects the corresponding vertices to
form a solid.  Here, each copy of the thread profile polygon is first
translated `r` units away from the central axis of the bolt and also
progressively further along the axis, based on the pitch `P`.  To that
is added a rotation around the axis, so that the composition of these
transformations forms a helix along which the thread profile is
extruded.

The final^[A concatenation of three transformations $A$, $B$, $C$ is
applied to each vertex $v$ of a polygon or polyhedron as $A \cdot B
\cdot C \cdot v$, so that the order of application is reversed.]
$90^\circ$ rotation merely serves to rotate the threads as a whole and
could just as well have been applied to the result of the extrusion
operation.  To illustrate that any transformation can be used in the
extrusion, a gradual scaling is included in the first revolution.
This tapers the thread towards the end of the bolt, to ease threading
into a nut.

The `draft` parameter is used here to select the number of steps for
each revolution of the thread, but its purpose and effect is much the
same as before.  Also, here too you can vary the diameter `d`, pitch
`P` and length `L`, to get any metric thread, or vary the profile
polygon to get other kinds of threads.  Obtaining a nut, is just a
matter of taking a difference with the hexagonal head instead of a
union.
